      "text": "To solve the given problem, we will follow the steps below:\n\n1. **Understanding the Geometry and Folding:**\n   - The square \\(ABCD\\) has sides \\(1\\) unit each.\n   - Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are on sides \\(AB\\) and \\(CB\\), respectively, such that \\(AE = CF\\).\n   - When the square is folded along the lines \\(DE\\) and \\(DF\\), sides \\(AD\\) and \\(CD\\) coincide on diagonal \\(BD\\).\n\n2. **Visualizing the Folded Shape:**\n   - After folding, points \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) coincide on diagonal \\(BD\\).\n   - The fold lines \\(DE\\) and \\(DF\\) ensure that \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) meet at a point, \\(P\\), which is the midpoint of \\(BD\\).\n\n3. **Setting Up the Coordinates:**\n   - Let \\(A = (0, 1)\\) and \\(B = (0, 0)\\).\n   - Since \\(AE = CF\\), point \\(E = (x_1, 1)\\) and \\(F = (1 - x_1, 0)\\), where \\(0 < x_1 < \\frac{1}{2}\\).\n\n4. **Finding the Midpoint \\(P\\) on Diagonal \\(BD\\):**\n   - The coordinates of \\(P\\) are \\(\\left(\\frac{x_1 + \\frac{1}{2}}{2}, \\frac{1 + 0}{2}\\right) = \\left(\\frac{x_1 + \\frac{1}{2}}{2}, \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\).\n\n5. **Using Similar Triangles:**\n   - Since \\(AD\\) and \\(CD\\) coincide on \\(BD\\), we can apply the properties of similar triangles.\n   - Let \\(AD\\) be folded such that it meets \\(BD\\) at point \\(Q\\). Because of the symmetry, \\(Q\\) is on the line \\(x = \\frac{1}{2}\\) and lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\).\n\n6. **Calculating the Length \\(AE\\):**\n   - The length of \\(AE\\) can be found using the Pythagorean theorem in the right triangle \\(AEG\\) where \\(E\\) is \\((x_1, 1)\\) and \\(G\\) is the midpoint of \\(AD\\).\n   - The coordinates of \\(G\\) are \\(\\left(\\frac{0 + 0}{2}, \\frac{1 + 1}{2}\\right) = (0, 1)\\).\n\n7. **Final Calculation:**\n   - The length \\(AE\\) is calculated as:\n     \\[\n     AE = \\sqrt{AG^2 - EG^2}\n     \\]\n     where \\(AG = \\frac{1}{2}\\) and \\(EG = 1 - \\frac{x_1}{2}\\).\n\nThus, the length of segment \\(AE